{
  "abstract": "Let $X$ be a projective hyper-Kähler fourfold of $K3^{[2]}$-type with $\\rho(X)=3$ whose transcendental Hodge structure has endomorphism field $\\mathbb Q$, and let $\\Sigma\\subset X$ be a smooth K3 surface on which the symplectic form does not vanish. Restriction multiplies the Beauville–Bogomolov form on $T(X)$ by an integer $c$, and a non-square $c$ gives an algebraic Hodge similarity that is not a scaled isometry. In earlier work we showed that $c$ is a square when $\\rho(X)\\le2$. We consider the case where the restriction $\\operatorname{NS}(X)_{\\mathbb Q}\\to\\operatorname{NS}(\\Sigma)_{\\mathbb Q}$ has rank one. Then $c\\in\\{3,7,8,12\\}$, so $c$ is never a square. If $X$ is a Bridgeland moduli space with equal nef and movable cones, then $c\\in\\{8,12\\}$; without that assumption, $c\\in\\{3,7\\}$ forces a non-Galois dominant rational map of degree $c$ from $\\Sigma$ onto a K3 surface. The proof uses Boucksom's divisorial Zariski decomposition and Markman's description of prime exceptional divisors, which show that $\\Sigma$ cannot lie in a contracted exceptional divisor. On Hilbert squares $S^{[2]}$ with $\\operatorname{NS}(S)=\\langle16\\rangle\\oplus\\langle-4\\rangle$, an $18$-dimensional family, we construct an integral Hodge class with $c=8$ together with the K3 lattice and Néron–Severi group that $\\Sigma$ would have. The class passes all the cohomological and positivity conditions we know. We do not know whether a surface in this class exists. On any Hilbert square, a surface with $c\\ge2$ does not lie in the divisor of subschemes meeting a smooth rational curve of $S$. A census of Hilbert squares with $\\rho(S)=2$ finds many more classes with $c=8$ and $c=12$.",
